.TH "BASH\-SERVER\-CLIENT\-PYTHON" "7" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server concepts"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Python client binding provides an async/await interface to the
bash\-server v2 NDJSON protocol.
It is implemented as the \f[CR]bashclient\f[R] package, requires Python
3.8 or later, and depends only on the Python standard library
(\f[CR]asyncio\f[R], \f[CR]base64\f[R], \f[CR]json\f[R],
\f[CR]socket\f[R], \f[CR]subprocess\f[R]).
.PP
The binding is fully asynchronous, built on \f[CR]asyncio\f[R].
All channel operations are coroutines that must be awaited.
A background reader task dispatches incoming messages to per\-channel
queues and registered callbacks.
.SH CONCEPTS
.SH Installation
The package is located at \f[CR]bash\-server/clients/python/\f[R] and
uses \f[CR]pyproject.toml\f[R] with setuptools as the build backend:
.IP
.EX
cd bash\-server/clients/python
pip install \-e .
.EE
.PP
For development with test dependencies:
.IP
.EX
pip install \-e \(dq.[dev]\(dq
.EE
.PP
This installs \f[CR]pytest>=7\f[R] and \f[CR]pytest\-asyncio>=0.21\f[R].
Tests are run with:
.IP
.EX
pytest
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]pyproject.toml\f[R] sets
\f[CR]asyncio_mode = \(dqauto\(dq\f[R] for pytest\-asyncio, so test
functions can use \f[CR]async def\f[R] directly without decorators.
.SH Package structure
.IP
.EX
bashclient/
    __init__.py      \-\- Package exports
    client.py        \-\- BashClient class
    channels.py      \-\- Six channel classes
    errors.py        \-\- Exception hierarchy
    protocol.py      \-\- NDJSON encode/decode, base64 helpers
    transport.py     \-\- Four transport implementations
    types.py         \-\- Dataclass definitions
.EE
.SH Architecture
The central class is \f[CR]BashClient\f[R].
It owns a \f[CR]Transport\f[R] instance and runs a background
\f[CR]asyncio.Task\f[R] (the reader loop) that continuously reads NDJSON
lines from the transport.
.PP
Incoming messages are routed by channel ID:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Request/response messages\f[R] are placed on per\-channel
\f[CR]asyncio.Queue\f[R] instances.
When a channel method sends a request, it awaits the corresponding queue
for the response.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Server\-push messages\f[R] (observe events, debug break_hit, PTY
output/exit) are dispatched directly to registered callbacks via the
channel\(cqs \f[CR]dispatch()\f[R] method.
.PP
Each channel is exposed as a public attribute on the client:
.IP
.EX
client.control   \f[I]# ControlChannel  (CHAN_CONTROL = 0)\f[R]
client.command   \f[I]# CommandChannel  (CHAN_COMMAND = 1)\f[R]
client.state     \f[I]# StateChannel    (CHAN_STATE = 2)\f[R]
client.observe   \f[I]# ObserveChannel  (CHAN_OBSERVE = 3)\f[R]
client.debug     \f[I]# DebugChannel    (CHAN_DEBUG = 4)\f[R]
client.pty       \f[I]# PtyChannel      (CHAN_PTY = 5)\f[R]
.EE
.SH Connection
\f[CR]BashClient\f[R] provides four class methods for establishing
connections, each returning a \f[CR]BashClient\f[R] instance with the
reader task already running:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Unix domain socket (default)\f[R]
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq)

\f[I]# Subprocess (\-\-stdio mode)\f[R]
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect_stdio(\(dqbash\-server\(dq, \(dq\-\-stdio\(dq)

\f[I]# Inherited file descriptor\f[R]
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect_fd(3)

\f[I]# Windows Named Pipe (Cygwin)\f[R]
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect_named_pipe(\(dq\(rs\(rs\(rs\(rs.\(rs\(rspipe\(rs\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq)

\f[I]# From existing transport\f[R]
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.from_transport(my_transport)
.EE
.PP
All factory methods are \f[CR]async\f[R] and must be awaited.
They instantiate the appropriate \f[CR]Transport\f[R] subclass, call its
\f[CR]connect()\f[R] method, and start the background reader.
.SH Async context manager
\f[CR]BashClient\f[R] supports \f[CR]async with\f[R] for automatic
cleanup:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]with\f[R] BashClient.connect(socket_path) \f[B]as\f[R] client:
    \f[B]await\f[R] client.auth(token)
    result = \f[B]await\f[R] client.eval(\(dqecho hello\(dq)
    print(result.stdout)
\f[I]# close() called automatically on exit\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]__aexit__\f[R] method sends a \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] message,
cancels the reader task, and closes the transport.
This is the recommended usage pattern.
.PP
Manual lifecycle is also supported:
.IP
.EX
client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect(socket_path)
\f[B]try\f[R]:
    \f[B]await\f[R] client.auth(token)
    \f[I]# ... use client ...\f[R]
\f[B]finally\f[R]:
    \f[B]await\f[R] client.close()
.EE
.SH Authentication
After connecting, the client must authenticate before using any channel
except CHAN_CONTROL:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]await\f[R] client.auth(token)
.EE
.PP
The token is a 64\-character hex string (256\-bit) generated by the
server at startup.
If authentication fails, \f[CR]AuthError\f[R] is raised.
.PP
The \f[CR]is_authenticated\f[R] property indicates whether
\f[CR]auth()\f[R] has completed successfully:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] client.is_authenticated:
    result = \f[B]await\f[R] client.eval(\(dqwhoami\(dq)
.EE
